<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Network policy for release builds of the IWM app.

  Everything the app talks to is HTTPS: the five AWS Lambda Function URLs and
  foodloss.ca. There is no reason for a cleartext socket to ever open, so the
  platform is told to refuse one rather than trusting that no code asks for it.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS WHEN THE DEFAULT IS ALREADY SECURE

  Since Android 9 (API 28) the platform default is already
  cleartextTrafficPermitted="false", so on paper this is redundant. It is here
  anyway for three reasons:

    * minSdk is 24, and on API 24 through 27 the default is the opposite.
      Those releases are a thin slice of installs, but they are exactly the
      ones where a protocol downgrade attack lands.

    * It is declarative, so a reviewer and an automated scanner can both read
      the policy without reading the app's networking code. "No explicit
      network security config" is a standard finding in Play's pre-launch
      report and in every MobSF run.

    * It makes the decision explicit for whoever next needs to debug against a
      local server. The debug variant of this file (src/debug/res/xml/) is the
      supported place to relax it, and it cannot affect what ships.

  User-installed CAs are excluded. Trusting them is what makes an interception
  proxy work on a device the attacker controls, and nothing here needs one.

  CERTIFICATE PINNING IS DELIBERATELY NOT CONFIGURED

  The endpoints are AWS Lambda Function URLs behind Amazon's own certificate
  rotation, and a pin set against a certificate we do not control is an outage
  waiting for a renewal. A bricked app in the field is a worse failure than the
  MITM it would prevent, given the traffic is already TLS 1.2+ on a
  publicly-trusted chain. If pinning is wanted later, pin the Amazon Root CA's
  SPKI rather than the leaf, and ship a backup pin.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
